      "id": "lauderdale",
      "name": "Lauderdale Tower",
      "type": "parts",
      "concept": "Serrated balconies, floor by floor.",
      "about": "The tower standing at the end of the lake, clear of the terraces. One of the Barbican's three residential towers, which dominate the City skyline at 42 storeys and about 123 m. Their jagged outline comes from balconies that wrap every floor, so the silhouette changes as you walk round.",
      "facts": [
        "The towers are triangular in plan, with serrated balcony silhouettes.",
        "Balcony edges are shaped like a boat hull, which reduces wind resistance.",
        "The topmost floors hold penthouse flats.",
        "Tip: turn each balcony slab 90 degrees from the one below to make the sawtooth edge."
